## Migration: Thread.Abort → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | `token.IsCancellationRequested` check |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` in Stop | Blocking wait | `thread.Join(timeout)` |

### Why This Breaks on .NET 8

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it cannot forcibly terminate threads. `ThreadAbortException` is never thrown. Your `Stop()` method will crash and the cleanup logic in the catch block will never execute.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Equivalent to the old ThreadAbortException handler
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — we control whether to continue or exit.
                // The loop condition will exit naturally.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        if (_workerThread is not null && _workerThread.IsAlive)
        {
            // Give thread time to finish gracefully
            if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)))
            {
                // Thread didn't exit — log a warning. You cannot force-kill it.
                // If this happens, GetNextItem is likely blocked on an
                // uncancellable call. See pitfalls below.
            }
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

### Critical: Unblock `GetNextItem`

The old code relied on `Thread.Abort` to interrupt a blocking `GetNextItem()`. In .NET 8, **you must make it cancellation-aware**:

```csharp
// If backed by a BlockingCollection<T>:
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException on cancel
}

// If backed by a manual wait:
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    // Replace: _signal.WaitOne()
    WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { _signal, token.WaitHandle });
    token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
    return DequeueItem();
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Impact |
|---------|--------|
| `GetNextItem` blocks without checking token | `Stop()` hangs forever — the thread never wakes up |
| Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource` | Timer/callback leaks |
| Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` to avoid masking unrelated exceptions |
| Assuming cancellation is instant | Only works at checkpoints — add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside long `ProcessItem` work |

### If `GetNextItem` Calls Unmodifiable Code

If you cannot make the blocking call cancellation-aware (e.g., third-party library), move it to a child process and use `Process.Kill()` as a last resort — this is the only way to forcibly terminate uncooperative code in modern .NET.
